For fun, let us write the number 2 as a fraction with denominator 5: \[ 2 = \frac{10}5.\]

So: \[ 2 \tfrac{1}{5}
\quad = \quad
2 + \frac{1}{5}
\quad = \quad
\frac{10}{5} + \frac{1}{5}
\quad = \quad
\frac{11}{5}.\]

We have written the mixed number $2 \tfrac{1}{5}$ as the improper fraction $\frac{11}{5}$.

Think about the addition problem $\frac 2 7 + \frac 3 7$:

\begin{align*}
\phantom{+}\text{ amount of pie each kid gets when 7 kids share 2 pies}\\
\underline{+ \text{ amount of pie each kid gets when 7 kids share 3 pies}}\\
\phantom{+} \text{?????} \qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad
\end{align*}



Since in both cases we have 7 kids sharing the pies, we can imagine that it is the same 7 kids in both cases.  First, they share 2 pies. Then they share 3 more pies. The total each child gets by the time all the pie-sharing is done is the same as if the 7 kids had just shared 5 pies to begin with. That is:

\begin{align*}
\phantom{+}\text{ amount of pie each kid gets when 7 kids share 2 pies}\\
\underline{+ \text{ amount of pie each kid gets when 7 kids share 3 pies}}\\
\phantom{+} \text{amount of pie each kid gets when 7 kids share 5 pies.}
\end{align*}



\[ \frac 2 7 + \frac 3 7 = \frac 5 7.\]

